> Can we allocate flush_bio in local stack? so that we don't need to fallback
> to submit_flush_wait_serial() for low memory case?

I tried the stack first, but the array is sized by MAX_DEVICES (8) and
each entry is a struct bio (136 bytes) plus a struct completion (88
bytes), so the frame grows to 1832 bytes and trips
-Wframe-larger-than=1024:

  fs/f2fs/segment.c: In function 'submit_flush_wait':
  fs/f2fs/segment.c:661:1: warning: the frame size of 1832 bytes is
  larger than 1024 bytes [-Wframe-larger-than=]

Since the allocation is small and bounded, how about keeping it on the
heap but allocating it with GFP_NOFS | __GFP_NOFAIL, which cannot fail?
That still lets us drop the serial fallback path entirely:

	flush_bio = kmalloc(array_size(sbi->s_ndevs, sizeof(*flush_bio)),
				GFP_NOFS | __GFP_NOFAIL);

v2 would also add REQ_SYNC to the flush bios to match what
submit_bio_wait() sets in the single-device blkdev_issue_flush() path.
